solution:
解 我们先讨论一种简单的情况。设一点电荷 $q_{1}$ 位于 $(0,0, a)$ 点，用镜像法，它的像电荷为在 $(0,0,-a)$ 点的 $q_{1}^{\prime}$ 和在 $(0,0, a)$ 点的 $q_{1}^{\prime \prime}$ ，注意，每一区域的势场由电荷 $q_{1}$ 和极化电荷（包括 $q_{1}$ 周围以及界面上的极化电荷)所激发，故两区域中电势分别为
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
\varphi_{1}=\frac{q}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{1} r_{1}}+\frac{q_{1}^{\prime}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r_{2}}, & z \geqslant 0 \\
\varphi_{2}=\frac{q}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{1} r_{1}}+\frac{q_{1}^{\prime \prime}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r_{1}}, & z<0
\end{array}
$$
式中
$$
r_{1}=\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}+(z-a)^{2}}, \quad r_{2}=\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}+(z+a)^{2}}
$$
利用界面 $(z=0)$ 上边界条件
$$
\varphi_{1}=\varphi_{2}, \quad \varepsilon_{1} \frac{\partial \varphi_{1}}{\partial z}=\varepsilon_{2} \frac{\partial \varphi_{2}}{\partial z}
$$
可得
$$
q_{1}^{\prime}=\frac{\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}} \cdot \frac{\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}} q_{1}, \quad q_{1}^{\prime \prime}=q_{1}^{\prime}
$$
类似地，如果在 $\varepsilon_{2}$ 介质中，在 $(0,0,-a)$ 处有一点电荷 $q_{2}$ ，则它的像电荷为 $q_{2}^{\prime}$ 在 $(0,0, a)$ 处， $q_{2}^{\prime \prime}$在 $(0,0,-a)$ 处，且有
$$
\begin{gathered}
\varphi_{1}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{2}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}}{r_{2}}+\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}^{\prime \prime}}{r_{2}}, \quad \varphi_{2}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{2}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}}{r_{2}}+\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}^{\prime}}{r_{1}} \\
q_{2}^{\prime}=\frac{\varepsilon_{1}}{\varepsilon_{2}} \cdot \frac{\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1}}{\varepsilon_{2}+\varepsilon_{1}} q_{2}, \quad q_{2}^{\prime \prime}=q_{2}^{\prime}
\end{gathered}
$$
当 $q_{1}, q_{2}$ 同时存在时， $q_{1}$ 所受的力应为 $q_{1}^{\prime}, q_{2}^{\prime \prime}$ 及 $q_{2}$ 的共同作用
$$
\begin{aligned}
F & =\frac{q_{1}}{4 \pi}\left[\frac{q_{2}}{\varepsilon_{2}(2 a)^{2}}+\frac{q_{1}^{\prime}}{\varepsilon_{0}(2 a)^{2}}+\frac{q_{2}^{*}}{\varepsilon_{0}(2 a)^{2}}\right] \\
& =\frac{1}{16 \pi a^{2}}\left[\frac{\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1} \varepsilon_{0}} \cdot \frac{\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}} q_{1}^{2}+\frac{1}{\varepsilon_{2}}\left(1+\frac{\varepsilon_{1}\left(\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1}\right)}{\varepsilon_{0}\left(\varepsilon_{2}+\varepsilon_{1}\right)}\right) q_{1} q_{2}\right]
\end{aligned}
$$
回到本题 $q_{1}=-q_{1} q_{2}=+q$ ，则有
$$
\begin{aligned}
F & =\frac{1}{16 \pi a^{2}}\left[\frac{\left(\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}\right)^{2}}{\varepsilon_{0} \varepsilon_{1} \varepsilon_{2}} q^{2}+\frac{\varepsilon_{1}}{\varepsilon_{2} \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}} q^{2}-\frac{q^{2}}{\varepsilon_{2}}\right] \\
& =\frac{-q^{2}}{16 \pi a^{2}} \frac{\varepsilon_{2}\left(\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}\right)}{\varepsilon_{0} \varepsilon_{1}\left(\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}\right)}-\frac{q^{2}}{16 \pi a^{2} \varepsilon_{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$
因此为使 $-q$ 静止不动，需在其上施加 $-F$ 的力.

Solution: Let's first discuss a simple case. Assume a point charge $q_{1}$ is located at point $(0,0, a)$. By using the method of images, its image charges are $q_{1}^{\prime}$ at $(0,0,-a)$ and $q_{1}^{\prime \prime}$ at $(0,0, a)$. Note that the potential field in each region is excited by the charge $q_{1}$ and the polarization charges (including those around $q_{1}$ and on the interface), so the potentials in the two regions are respectively

$$
\begin{array}{ll}
\varphi_{1}=\frac{q}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{1} r_{1}}+\frac{q_{1}^{\prime}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r_{2}}, & z \geqslant 0 \\
\varphi_{2}=\frac{q}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{1} r_{1}}+\frac{q_{1}^{\prime \prime}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r_{1}}, & z<0
\end{array}
$$

where

$$
r_{1}=\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}+(z-a)^{2}}, \quad r_{2}=\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}+(z+a)^{2}}
$$

Using the boundary conditions at the interface $(z=0)$

$$
\varphi_{1}=\varphi_{2}, \quad \varepsilon_{1} \frac{\partial \varphi_{1}}{\partial z}=\varepsilon_{2} \frac{\partial \varphi_{2}}{\partial z}
$$

we obtain

$$
q_{1}^{\prime}=\frac{\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}} \cdot \frac{\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}} q_{1}, \quad q_{1}^{\prime \prime}=q_{1}^{\prime}
$$

Similarly, if in the medium $\varepsilon_{2}$ there is a point charge $q_{2}$ at $(0,0,-a)$, its image charges are $q_{2}^{\prime}$ at $(0,0, a)$ and $q_{2}^{\prime \prime}$ at $(0,0,-a)$, with

$$
\begin{gathered}
\varphi_{1}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{2}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}}{r_{2}}+\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}^{\prime \prime}}{r_{2}}, \quad \varphi_{2}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{2}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}}{r_{2}}+\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \cdot \frac{q_{2}^{\prime}}{r_{1}} \\
q_{2}^{\prime}=\frac{\varepsilon_{1}}{\varepsilon_{2}} \cdot \frac{\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1}}{\varepsilon_{2}+\varepsilon_{1}} q_{2}, \quad q_{2}^{\prime \prime}=q_{2}^{\prime}
\end{gathered}
$$

When both $q_{1}$ and $q_{2}$ exist simultaneously, the force on $q_{1}$ is due to the combined effect of $q_{1}^{\prime}$, $q_{2}^{\prime \prime}$, and $q_{2}$

$$
\begin{aligned}
F & =\frac{q_{1}}{4 \pi}\left[\frac{q_{2}}{\varepsilon_{2}(2 a)^{2}}+\frac{q_{1}^{\prime}}{\varepsilon_{0}(2 a)^{2}}+\frac{q_{2}^{*}}{\varepsilon_{0}(2 a)^{2}}\right] \\
& =\frac{1}{16 \pi a^{2}}\left[\frac{\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1} \varepsilon_{0}} \cdot \frac{\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}} q_{1}^{2}+\frac{1}{\varepsilon_{2}}\left(1+\frac{\varepsilon_{1}\left(\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1}\right)}{\varepsilon_{0}\left(\varepsilon_{2}+\varepsilon_{1}\right)}\right) q_{1} q_{2}\right]
\end{aligned}
$$

Returning to this problem with $q_{1}=-q_{1} q_{2}=+q$, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
F & =\frac{1}{16 \pi a^{2}}\left[\frac{\left(\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}\right)^{2}}{\varepsilon_{0} \varepsilon_{1} \varepsilon_{2}} q^{2}+\frac{\varepsilon_{1}}{\varepsilon_{2} \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}}{\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}} q^{2}-\frac{q^{2}}{\varepsilon_{2}}\right] \\
& =\frac{-q^{2}}{16 \pi a^{2}} \frac{\varepsilon_{2}\left(\varepsilon_{1}-\varepsilon_{2}\right)}{\varepsilon_{0} \varepsilon_{1}\left(\varepsilon_{1}+\varepsilon_{2}\right)}-\frac{q^{2}}{16 \pi a^{2} \varepsilon_{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, to keep $-q$ stationary, a force of $-F$ needs to be applied to it.

