$$

The speed and acceleration of projectile motion. For a particle undergoing projectile motion, it is only subject to the force of gravity, so its total acceleration is the gravitational acceleration, directed vertically downward. At the highest point of the trajectory, its velocity has only a horizontal component, with a magnitude equal to the horizontal component of the initial velocity. Therefore, the tangential acceleration is zero at this point, and the normal acceleration is equal to $g$. Thus, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
a_{n}=g & =\frac{v^{2}}{\rho}=\frac{v_{0}^{2} \cos ^{2} \theta}{\rho} \\
\rho & =\frac{v_{0}^{2} \cos ^{2} \theta}{g}
