The frequency of wave is equal to the ratio of speed of the wave and wavelength of the wave. 
$$v=\frac{v}{\lambda}$$
Here, $v$ is the frequency, $v$ is the speed, and $\lambda$ is the wavelength.

Rearrange the above equation for wavelength:

$$
\lambda=\frac{v}{v}
$$

The speed of sound in air is constant and equal to $330 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}$. Substitute $330 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}$ for $v$ and 20 Hz for $v$ in the above equation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda & =\frac{330 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}}{20 \mathrm{~Hz}} \\
& =16.5 \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute $330 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}$ for $v$ and 20000 Hz for $v$ in the above equation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda & =\frac{330 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}}{20000 \mathrm{~Hz}} \\
& =0.0165 \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus, the wavelength range is $16.5 \mathrm{~m}>\lambda>0.0165 \mathrm{~m}$.

